SAFE COLD EMAIL OUTREACH EXECUTION ENGINE
you’d be operating in the same category and with the same underlying execution model as tools like Snov.io, SendGrid/Mailgun-style SMTP senders, Sendgreen, and Brevo: users bring or buy mailboxes/domains, authenticate them via SMTP/IMAP, and your SaaS centrally schedules, throttles, warms, and monitors outbound emails on shared infrastructure while replies land directly in the user’s real inbox (webmail/Gmail/etc.); the difference between “safe SaaS” and “spam tool” isn’t the tech stack but how conservative your limits, warm-up, authentication enforcement, queueing, and kill-switches are, which is exactly how those platforms stay deliverable and compliant.
Role & Objective
You are designing a Cold outbound email execution system for a SaaS platform.
The system must:
Maximize deliverability
Remain within industry-accepted safety zones
Avoid spam classification
Protect shared IP reputation
Be deterministic, auditable, and boring (no “growth hacks”)
This system does not attempt to bypass provider safeguards.
It operates within accepted SMTP norms and compliance frameworks.

Core Principles (Non-Negotiable)
Reputation > Volume
Predictability > Speed
Provider rules are constraints, not obstacles
Shared IP = shared responsibility
Automation must behave like disciplined humans

Architectural Overview
Execution Model
Centralized Outbound Mail Dispatcher
Stateless send workers
Single or small pool of shared static IPs
Users authenticate their mailbox via SMTP credentials
System sends on behalf of users, never impersonates domains

IP SHARING STRATEGY (SAFE ZONE)
IP Pool Rules
IPs are shared across users
Each IP has:
Global hourly cap
Global daily cap
Reputation score
Cooldown state
Example Safe Envelope (Conservative)
Metric
Value
Emails per IP / day
500–1,000
Emails per IP / hour
≤50 (will try to minimize if possible)
Concurrent SMTP sessions
≤3-5 (will manage in a way tht no multiple email should not be send same minutes! Will try to send max 1 per 1 minute! If possible)
Burst sending
❌ Never
Nighttime idle
Required

If the IP approaches limits → change the IP! E.g. If user campaign is set to 200 email per day but the ip capacity left is under 200 then shift tht campaign to new IP! Dont split it between different ips! 

SMTP Execution Rules
Mandatory SMTP Capabilities
STARTTLS
AUTH LOGIN / PLAIN
RFC-compliant headers
Proper HELO hostname
Reverse DNS aligned with IP
SMTP Behavior
One message per transaction
New SMTP connection every N messages
Randomized delay between sends (human-like pacing)
No retries on hard failures
Soft failures go to delayed retry queue

Authentication & Trust Chain
Required (Hard Gate)
SPF aligned
DKIM signed
DMARC present (p=none minimum)
If any fail:
Account enters restricted mode
Sending throttled to near-zero
User notified with diagnostics

Warm-Up Logic (Provider-Agnostic)
Warm-up is automatic, invisible, and enforced.
Warm-Up States
Cold
Warming
Stable
Throttled
Suspended
Progression is time-based + behavior-based, not volume-based.

Message Quality Guardrails
The system must enforce:
Plain-text first (HTML optional, light)
No URL shorteners
No tracking pixels by default
Max 1–2 links
No deceptive subject lines
No aggressive CTA patterns
AI-generated content must pass:
Spam-phrase linting
Over-personalization checks
Repetition detection

Reply & Bounce Handling
Replies
All replies go directly to user mailbox
SaaS only listens via IMAP (read-only)
Replies update campaign state (manual or automated)
Bounces
Hard bounce → permanent suppression
Soft bounce → retry with delay
Repeated soft → suppression
Bounce rate thresholds:
3% → throttle
5% → pause account
8% → suspend sending

Compliance & Safety Layer
Required Controls
Global unsubscribe list
Per-user suppression list
Per-campaign suppression
Automatic unsubscribe header
Footer compliance (jurisdiction-agnostic)
No sending allowed if:
User has no unsubscribe mechanism
Campaign targets scraped emails without justification metadata

Queue & Scheduling System
Scheduler Rules
FIFO with priority weights
Campaigns spread across hours
No simultaneous large campaigns
Per-domain pacing enforced
No “send now” button that bypasses limits. Ever.

Observability & Kill Switches
Real-Time Metrics
IP reputation score
Bounce rate (rolling)
Reply ratio
Spam complaint signals (if available)
SMTP error frequency
Automatic Kill Switch
Triggers:
Sudden bounce spike
Repeated SMTP rejections
Provider temp blocks
Action:
Immediate pause
Gradual recovery only

What This System Explicitly Does NOT Do
❌ No inbox spoofing
❌ No provider rule bypassing
❌ No fingerprint manipulation
❌ No shadow IP rotation
❌ No sending without auth
❌ No scraping emails

Scaling Philosophy
Scaling happens by:
Adding more IPs slowly
Increasing time, not rate
Improving reply quality, not volume
100k/month is achieved by:
Many days
Many hours
Many small, safe sends

### ⚙️ Production Background Worker (24/7 Execution)

To run the email engine continuously without keeping the browser tab open:

1. **Start the Worker:**
   Open a terminal in the project folder and run:
   ```bash
   node background-worker.js
   ```
   *Tip: Use tools like `pm2` or `forever` to keep it running after restarts.*

2. **What it does:**
   - Connects directly to MongoDB (bypassing the web server).
   - Checks for "Active" campaigns every 60 seconds.
   - Respects all safety limits, proxy bindings, and time windows.
   - Logs activity to the console.

3. **Troubleshooting:**
   - If a campaign isn't sending, check the console output. It will tell you exactly why (e.g., "Outside hours", "Daily Limit", "No Proxy").

